#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:574
msgid "Examples"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:576
msgid ""
"To build the dependencies of the package Pan, and start an interactive shell "
"in which to build it:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:589
msgid ""
"The reason we use form `eval ${configurePhase:-configurePhase}` here is "
"because those packages that override these phases do so by exporting the "
"overridden values in the environment variable of the same name. Here bash is "
"being told to either evaluate the contents of 'configurePhase', if it exists "
"as a variable, otherwise evaluate the configurePhase function."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:595
msgid ""
"To clear the environment first, and do some additional automatic "
"initialisation of the interactive shell:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:603
msgid ""
"Nix expressions can also be given on the command line using the `-E` and `-"
"p` flags. For instance, the following starts a shell containing the packages "
"`sqlite` and `libX11`:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:607
msgid ""
"```console\n"
"$ nix-shell --expr 'with import <nixpkgs> { }; runCommand "
"\"dummy\" { buildInputs = [ sqlite xorg.libX11 ]; } \"\"'\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:611
msgid "A shorter way to do the same is:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:619
msgid ""
"Note that `-p` accepts multiple full nix expressions that are valid in the "
"`buildInputs = [ ... ]` shown above, not only package names. So the "
"following is also legal:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:627
msgid ""
"The `-p` flag looks up Nixpkgs in the Nix search path. You can override it "
"by passing `-I` or setting `NIX_PATH`. For example, the following gives you "
"a shell containing the Pan package from a specific revision of Nixpkgs:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:632
msgid ""
"```console\n"
"$ nix-shell --packages pan -I nixpkgs=https://github.com/NixOS/nixpkgs/"
"archive/8a3eea054838b55aca962c3fbde9c83c102b8bf2.tar.gz\n"
"\n"
"[nix-shell:~]$ pan --version\n"
"Pan 0.139\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:639
msgid "Use as a `#!`\\-interpreter"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:641
msgid ""
"You can use `nix-shell` as a script interpreter to allow scripts written in "
"arbitrary languages to obtain their own dependencies via Nix. This is done "
"by starting the script with the following lines:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:646 source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:701
msgid "#! /usr/bin/env nix-shell\n"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:650
msgid ""
"where _real-interpreter_ is the “real” script interpreter that will be "
"invoked by `nix-shell` after it has obtained the dependencies and "
"initialised the environment, and _packages_ are the attribute names of the "
"dependencies in Nixpkgs."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:655
msgid ""
"The lines starting with `#! nix-shell` specify `nix-shell` options (see "
"above). Note that you cannot write `#! /usr/bin/env nix-shell -i ...` "
"because many operating systems only allow one argument in `#!` lines."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:659
msgid ""
"For example, here is a Python script that depends on Python and the "
"`prettytable` package:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:663
msgid ""
"#! /usr/bin/env nix-shell\n"
"#! nix-shell -i python3 --packages python3 python3Packages.prettytable\n"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:667
msgid "# Print a simple table.\n"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:669
msgid "\"N\""
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:669
msgid "\"N^2\""
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:674
msgid ""
"Similarly, the following is a Perl script that specifies that it requires "
"Perl and the `HTML::TokeParser::Simple`, `LWP` and `LWP::Protocol::Https` "
"packages:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:679
msgid ""
"#! /usr/bin/env nix-shell\n"
"#! nix-shell -i perl \n"
"#! nix-shell --packages perl \n"
"#! nix-shell --packages perlPackages.HTMLTokeParserSimple \n"
"#! nix-shell --packages perlPackages.LWP\n"
"#! nix-shell --packages perlPackages.LWPProtocolHttps\n"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:687
msgid "# Fetch nixos.org and print all hrefs.\n"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:689
msgid "'https://nixos.org/'"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:691 source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:729
msgid "\"a\""
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:692 source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:730
msgid "\"href\""
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:693
msgid "\"$href\\n\""
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:697
msgid ""
"Sometimes you need to pass a simple Nix expression to customize a package "
"like Terraform:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:702
msgid "'terraform.withPlugins (plugins: [ plugins.openstack ])'"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:709
msgid ""
"You must use single or double quotes (`'`, `\"`) when passing a simple Nix "
"expression in a nix-shell shebang."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:712
msgid ""
"Finally, using the merging of multiple nix-shell shebangs the following "
"Haskell script uses a specific branch of Nixpkgs/NixOS (the 20.03 stable "
"branch):"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:718
msgid ""
"--packages 'haskellPackages.ghcWithPackages (ps: [ps.download-curl ps."
"tagsoup])'\n"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:725
msgid "-- Fetch nixos.org and print all hrefs.\n"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:728
msgid "\"https://nixos.org/\""
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:731
msgid "\"\""
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:734
msgid ""
"If you want to be even more precise, you can specify a specific revision of "
"Nixpkgs:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:737
msgid ""
"    #! nix-shell -I nixpkgs=https://github.com/NixOS/nixpkgs/"
"archive/0672315759b3e15e2121365f067c1c8c56bb4722.tar.gz\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:739
msgid ""
"The examples above all used `-p` to get dependencies from Nixpkgs. You can "
"also use a Nix expression to build your own dependencies. For example, the "
"Python example could have been written as:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:744
msgid ""
"#! /usr/bin/env nix-shell\n"
"#! nix-shell deps.nix -i python\n"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:748
msgid ""
"where the file `deps.nix` in the same directory as the `#!`\\-script "
"contains:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:751
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"with import <nixpkgs> {};\n"
"\n"
"runCommand \"dummy\" { buildInputs = [ python3 python3Packages."
"prettytable ]; } \"\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:757
msgid ""
"The script's file name is passed as the first argument to the interpreter "
"specified by the `-i` flag."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:759
msgid ""
"Aside from the very first line, which is a directive to the operating "
"system, the additional `#! nix-shell` lines do not need to be at the "
"beginning of the file. This allows wrapping them in block comments for "
"languages where `#` does not start a comment, such as ECMAScript, Erlang, or "
"PHP."
msgstr ""
